UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 22, Lesson 1: Millimetres and kilometres', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Millimetres measure short lengths (10 mm = 1 cm). Kilometres measure long distances (1000 m = 1 km). Match the unit to the size.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nChoose mm, cm, m or km: thickness of a coin; length of a pencil; length of a classroom; distance between towns. Convert 40 mm to cm.\n\nFEEDBACK\nmm; cm; m; km. 40 mm = 4 cm.\n\nWATCH FOR\nUsing kilometres for a pencil length is the wrong scale.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nSort twelve everyday lengths into mm/cm/m/km. Convert six measures between mm↔cm and m↔km. Write one sentence about why unit choice matters.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Millimetres measure short lengths (10 mm = 1 cm). Kilometres measure long distances (1000 m = 1 km). Match the unit to the size.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Concept","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-GM-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Sort twelve everyday lengths into mm/cm/m/km. Convert six measures between mm↔cm and m↔km. Write one sentence about why unit choice matters. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: mm; cm; m; km. 40 mm = 4 cm. Reasoning: I used millimetre by attending to kilometre and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Using kilometres for a pencil length is the wrong scale. Correction: mm; cm; m; km. 40 mm = 4 cm. Why: the correction restores accurate use of millimetre.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Using kilometres for a pencil length is the wrong scale. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses millimetre with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with millimetre, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values. / Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"mm; cm; m; km. 40 mm = 4 cm. This matches the lesson skill for millimetre.","options":["Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","mm; cm; m; km. 40 mm = 4 cm.","Using kilometres for a pencil length is the wrong scale."],"question":"Choose mm, cm, m or km: thickness of a coin; length of a pencil; length of a classroom; distance between towns. Convert 40 mm to cm. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Using kilometres for a pencil length is the wrong scale. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["mm; cm; m; km. 40 mm = 4 cm.","Careful use of kilometre and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Using kilometres for a pencil length is the wrong scale.","Millimetres measure short lengths (10 mm = 1 cm). Kilometres measure long distances (1000 m = 1 km). Match the unit to the size."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising millimetre?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named kilometre and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the kilometre and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact."],"question":"A learner is practising millimetre. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value or quantity.","options":["Using kilometres for a pencil length is the wrong scale.","Show the places, values or steps and say why each step is valid.","Write only a final number with no working.","Change digits to look neater without checking place value."],"question":"Which working note best supports a mathematics answer about millimetre?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the method; the others break it.","options":["Using kilometres for a pencil length is the wrong scale.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","mm; cm; m; km. 40 mm = 4 cm."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Millimetres measure short lengths (10 mm = 1 cm). Kilometres measure long distances (1000 m = 1 km). Match… — which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus.","options":["Average the two answers without checking either.","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed.","Pick the longer-looking working automatically.","Delete units so the answers appear to match."],"question":"Which error should you repair first if two methods disagree?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same method.","options":["Retype the worked model numbers as the answer.","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Solve a fresh problem with different numbers and explain the method."],"question":"Which practice choice shows transfer of millimetre?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Useful feedback names the failed step and shows corrected working.","options":["Praise neat handwriting even when the places are wrong.","Tell the learner to skip working and only keep the final digits.","Just add more digits until it looks longer.","Name the exact place or step that failed, then show corrected working for millimetre."],"question":"Which feedback comment is most useful after a mistake?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses millimetre with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: mm; cm; m; km. 40 mm = 4 cm. Reasoning: I used millimetre by attending to kilometre and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with millimetre, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Using kilometres for a pencil length is the wrong scale. Correction: mm; cm; m; km. 40 mm = 4 cm. Why: the correction restores accurate use of millimetre.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y3-w22-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise millimetre.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Measurement and Space","success_criteria":["I can use millimetre with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify kilometre and explain why it matters.","I can practise scale independently on a new prompt.","I can notice unit needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Millimetres measure short lengths (10 mm = 1 cm). Kilometres measure long distances (1000 m = 1 km). Match the unit to the size. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no working.","Millimetres measure short lengths (10 mm = 1 cm). Kilometres measure long distances (1000 m = 1 km). Match the unit to the size.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about millimetre?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA tiny seed gap might be 3 mm. A road trip might be 12 km. 25 mm = 2 cm 5 mm. 3000 m = 3 km.\n\nTRY FIRST\nChoose mm, cm, m or km: thickness of a coin; length of a pencil; length of a classroom; distance between towns. Convert 40 mm to cm.\n\nFEEDBACK\nmm; cm; m; km. 40 mm = 4 cm.","explain":"Millimetres measure short lengths (10 mm = 1 cm). Kilometres measure long distances (1000 m = 1 km). Match the unit to the size.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Using kilometres for a pencil length is the wrong scale. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Millimetres and kilometres on a scale of size.","caption":"Match unit to the length.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">mm</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">10 mm = 1 cm</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">km</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">1000 m = 1 km</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: A tiny seed gap might be 3 mm. A road trip might be 12 km. 25 mm = 2 cm 5 mm. 3000 m = 3 km. Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: A tiny seed gap might be 3 mm. A road trip might be 12 km. 25 mm = 2 cm 5 mm. 3000 m = 3 km. Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about millimetre?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA tiny seed gap might be 3 mm. A road trip might be 12 km. 25 mm = 2 cm 5 mm. 3000 m = 3 km. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Every